WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.420
Almost two years ago, I made
a video about fire marks.

00:00:03.420 --> 00:00:04.620
Back in the 18th century,

00:00:04.620 --> 00:00:07.380
firefighters in London were
funded by insurance companies.

00:00:07.380 --> 00:00:10.320
When you insured a building,
you would be given a fire mark,

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.760
a big plaque to put on the front

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:13.200
And if a fire ever broke out,

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:16.080
all the various companies' firefighters
would rush to the scene.

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:18.600
and the story goes that
they would look at the building,

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.080
and only fight the fire
if they saw their company's mark,

00:00:22.080 --> 00:00:24.360
or the mark of a company that
they had an agreement with.

00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.960
"If the building wasn't insured,
they would let it burn."

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:30.840
This is a really well-known
story in Britain,

00:00:30.840 --> 00:00:33.060
it's part of the standard
'history of the fire brigades'.

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:35.160
Panel show QI has talked about it.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:37.650
"If you hadn't paid, they wouldn't
put your fire out.

00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:40.080
"Unless you happened to be next
to someone else who had."

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:43.200
Children's TV show Horrible Histories
did a whole sketch on it

00:00:43.200 --> 00:00:45.000
including the host,
a talking rat,

00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:47.760
holding up a sign that says
'they really did this'.

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:51.540
And the sources I used for my video
included the history page

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.360
on the London Fire Brigade Museum's
own website.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.760
This was not controversial.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:01.260
Now, soon after publication, I was told
about one embarrassing error in the video:

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:04.080
the plaques I filmed weren't
actually fire marks.

00:01:04.080 --> 00:01:06.240
There's no list of all the
surviving marks anywhere,

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.720
but I found stock photos of some marks
with locations attached,

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:12.060
and I figured, great,
I can just film there.

00:01:12.060 --> 00:01:13.920
But, those captions are wrong,

00:01:13.920 --> 00:01:15.720
whoever wrote them
was completely mistaken,

00:01:15.720 --> 00:01:17.940
apparently those are
parish boundary markers.

00:01:17.940 --> 00:01:20.640
Which is not great, but it
s the sort of thing I can fix

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:21.540
by pinning a correction,

00:01:21.540 --> 00:01:24.120
it doesn't affect whether the
main part of the video is true.

00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:27.480
But. About a month ago,
I got an email that was like,

00:01:27.480 --> 00:01:30.900
you know that source you used,
the London Fire Brigade Museum's page?

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.820
It doesn't say that.

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:36.600
And it doesn't. The page has
been changed, and it now says:

00:01:36.600 --> 00:01:40.080
"There is little real evidence to
suggest that this was the case."

00:01:40.080 --> 00:01:43.440
So I was straight off into
a rabbit hole of research.

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.080
Because this was exactly on
a weird borderline in my head:

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.640
at times I was like,
well, actually,

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.380
that makes no sense
when you think about it,

00:01:49.380 --> 00:01:52.320
no-one would let a fire burn if
they were standing there with water.

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:53.340
It's got to be an urban legend.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:54.660
And then I'd switch to,

00:01:54.660 --> 00:01:58.440
well, there were many more cruel
things going on in that century.

00:01:58.440 --> 00:02:00.660
Heck, there still are, there are
places in the world

00:02:00.660 --> 00:02:04.320
where that system exists today,
it's summed up as "no pay, no spray".

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.580
And it seemed like every source I read
had a slightly different opinion.

00:02:08.580 --> 00:02:10.920
I couldn't find any actual
testimonials from the time,

00:02:10.920 --> 00:02:13.260
and the secondary and tertiary
sources that I had access to

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:14.820
didn't go into any depth:

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:17.580
most of them just briefly stated it
as an unreferenced fact

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:18.480
and said no more.

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:21.000
I'm on the road right now,
so I can’t go into archives,

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:23.040
and even if I could,
I wouldn't know where to start.

00:02:23.580 --> 00:02:28.320
So: I hired an experienced, professional
archives research consultant.

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:31.200
My brief was simple:
is the story true?

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:33.660
Answering that took the consultant
two weeks of work.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:35.640
Not two weeks of
undergrad-slacking-off-time,

00:02:35.640 --> 00:02:38.160
I mean two solid weeks of
eight-hour work days,

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.480
including visiting the
British Library

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:41.760
to check documents that
haven't been digitised yet.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.340
He's put together a
thoroughly-referenced report,

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:45.840
I've linked it down
in the description.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.160
And now I'm going to summarise
his summary in about 60 seconds.

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:50.700
Here we go.

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:53.880
Through the 18th century,
fire marks were an indicator

00:02:53.880 --> 00:02:55.560
of whether a building was insured,

00:02:55.560 --> 00:02:58.260
but the general policy of
London insurance companies

00:02:58.260 --> 00:03:01.620
was that their firefighters
did try to fight all fires,

00:03:01.620 --> 00:03:04.200
whether insured or not.
Three reasons why:

00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.720
One. A fire in an
un-insured building

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.700
can easily spread
to an insured property,

00:03:08.700 --> 00:03:10.200
or dozens of insured properties.

00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:12.660
Economically, it made sense
to cut the problem off

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:13.560
as early as possible.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:18.420
Two. It is really good advertising
to have smartly dressed firefighters

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:20.100
rushing in to save the day.

00:03:20.100 --> 00:03:23.340
And three: it was the
right thing to do.

00:03:23.340 --> 00:03:26.640
There are good references
from the time to back all that up.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:31.740
But. By 1721, there were eight
fire insurance offices in London.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:33.720
We don’t know how many actual
fire brigades there were,

00:03:33.720 --> 00:03:36.780
but there were enough for there
to be rivalries between them.

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.120
And there was also a law
that gave reward money

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:41.940
to the first, second, and third
brigades to attend a fire.

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.120
That's one of the reasons
they raced to fires so fast,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.160
they got paid more if they did.

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.640
So sometimes, too many firefighters
would turn up. They couldn't all help,

00:03:50.640 --> 00:03:53.460
and there might not be enough
fire-fighting water for all of them.

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:56.280
Many of those firefighters were
recruited from being watermen

00:03:56.280 --> 00:03:58.380
on the River Thames,
rough-and-ready ferry workers.

00:03:58.380 --> 00:04:01.440
So if they weren’t quick enough,
and they missed out on the reward,

00:04:01.440 --> 00:04:03.900
and it wasn't a building
insured by their company anyway,

00:04:03.900 --> 00:04:07.260
they might just stand back,
jeer a bit, or worst case,

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:09.120
maybe even interfere with the folks

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:11.280
who were going to get the
reward money instead of them.

00:04:11.820 --> 00:04:13.440
That is, in the researcher's view,

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.720
the most probable place
that the story came from.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:18.120
There are so many more
subtleties than that,

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:20.400
this is not certain,
history is fractal

00:04:20.400 --> 00:04:22.980
and you can always, always find
more detail if you keep looking.

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.340
We're summarising more
than 150 years here.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:29.820
But big picture: did some firefighters
ever stand back

00:04:29.820 --> 00:04:30.960
and let a building burn?

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.120
Yes, it probably did happen.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:35.040
But there's no evidence
from the time to show it

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:38.400
was ever an official, standard
policy for insurance fire brigades

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:39.360
in 18th century London.

00:04:39.360 --> 00:04:42.480
I was wrong. QI was wrong.
Horrible Histories was wrong.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:46.680
Hundreds, maybe thousands, of pop-history
books and storytellers were wrong.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:48.293
We think.

00:04:48.293 --> 00:04:50.520
If any history students are
looking for a PhD topic,

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:53.040
it feels like you could get
three years of research out of this

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:55.200
and still not end up
with a definitive yes or no.

00:04:55.200 --> 00:04:58.020
It's a perfect example of
how quickly and how badly

00:04:58.020 --> 00:05:00.300
even big important stories
can become muddled,

00:05:00.300 --> 00:05:03.240
and how the study of history
is not about memorising dates:

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:06.540
it's about the interpretation
of really messy, patchy data.

00:05:06.540 --> 00:05:09.780
So I have pulled my original video.
It's unlisted now.

00:05:09.780 --> 00:05:11.520
I've put a link in the description
here for posterity,

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:12.900
but YouTube won't
ever recommend it,

00:05:12.900 --> 00:05:14.100
it won't show up
in search results,

00:05:14.100 --> 00:05:15.540
and I've put a big
warning on it.

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.720
Also: turns out I made
another error anyway,

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:20.820
I said the wrong organisation name
later on in the video.

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.560
As ever, you shouldn't trust me.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:25.800
Sometimes I blunder, sometimes I'm
standing on the shoulders

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:27.000
of mistaken giants.

00:05:27.000 --> 00:05:29.760
Anyone who tells you they're certain
about how the world works

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:32.880
is either a mathematician,
or they're selling something.

